<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> Yes</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP Article</p>
<p><strong>Genre (Optional):</strong> building / architecture</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> A restroom that is fully functional while not being connected to the public infrastructure. It makes its users want to use it more often.</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong> The SCP consists of a restroom for males and an adjacant one for females, located in an office building. Both are built and working as expected (2 sinks, 4 stalls, 4 additional urinals for the male section). During a planned reconstruction it is discovered that the restroom has never been connected to public water supply and the sewers. Therefore it is unclear where the water actually comes from and where it goes. While there is piping below the restrooms, there is no access from the sewers / public infrastructure.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Everyone previously working there has no knowledge of this - but they have rather fond memories of it, describing to using it quite often. During questioning people having used the SCP regularly express an urge to use it again. Strange behaviour includes brushing teeth and complete body hygene at the sink, flushing of office documents, a wedding ring and an unknown amount of drugs. They also admit using the restroom when not necessary, just letting water flow for minutes or flushing down unused toilet paper.<br />
The Foundation takes over the site and conducts research experiments.</p>
<ul>
<li>water sample analysis (conclusion: regular US tap water)</li>
<li>mobile camera in sewer (conclusion: non-liquid materials are destroyed at the edge of SCP piping)</li>
<li>tracer in sewage (hint: might lead to an ocean thousands of miles away)</li>
<li>self-testing of the research staff shows incresed endorphine levels after experiments directly involving the SCPs</li>
</ul>
<p>How does the SCP work? It takes water from an unknown source (one or several) and discards it towards an unknown location, thereby acting like some kind of digestive tract (sounds gross, I know). People who regularly use the SCP become somewhat addicted, wanting to go back and use it again (no actual harm other than this). EDIT: improved on the &quot;digestive tract&quot; idea, making it part of something larger, which is unknown<br />
EDIT: Containment: originally simply guarding it and restricting access. surface cleaning of the rooms only (no running water). cleaning crews report of feeling hungry and hearing &quot;growling&quot; (think stomach). after an incident this is then adjusted<br />
Incident: a man passes security to use the restrooms which were unused for months. when he flushes there is a rather ugly eruption from all stalls. During the incident a minor earth quake occoured at tracer-experiment-ocean.<br />
<strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber:</strong> The idea is an everyday location one doesn't put much thought in. I want to focus on research rather than actual anomalus behaviour (after comments I added anomalous behaviour). This would include audio transcripts, post-experiment reports and so forth. Due to the rigorous containment an incident occours.</p>
<p><strong>Additional Notes:</strong> Maybe the idea is dull? EDIT: Maybe its gross now?</p>
