														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4797310">
									

<p>Hello, I'm Riemann and I'm here on behalf of <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/naveil" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5986482); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=5986482&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1602865951" alt="Naveil" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=5986482)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/naveil" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5986482); return false;">Naveil</a></span> and the Furretsidence!</p>
<p>I rather like this newer updated concept. It hints at a larger story that we're not privy to - <em>why</em> does this exist.</p>
<p>I think this is exemplary of the strength of the SCP format - a thing that exists in the world that we do not entirely understand, and leaves us with a couple of burning questions.</p>
<p>I would suggest a couple things here. First, understand the scope of this idea. It's small and harmless, and I think that works to its advantage. It's ok to have things that just&#8230; exist and are largely unexplained. It doesn't need to be anything it's not. I even like the <em>minor</em> compulsion effect, but I don't think the article should focus on it. Just state these things as they are - people seem to just like running the water in there.</p>
<p>To that end, don't drag it out too much. Think about which parts of the idea aid the story you're trying to tell, and which parts don't. How many test logs do you need? What do they add?</p>
<p>And finally, I think you've nailed the idea that we don't fully understand this, but you, as the author, need to know what's going on. You should have the complete description in your head so that you are best able to cut away pieces and information to present this story. The appeal here is that there <em>is</em> a complete story somewhere, but we do not know the full information and we never will. This anomaly is <em>doing</em> something, but it's just out of sight for us.</p>
<p>I'm going to <span style="color: green"><strong>greenlight</strong></span> this, hoping that you follow the above advice.</p>

