<p><span style="text-decoration: line-through;">Summoned via PM, expect crit within 24 hours.</span></p>
<p>As an aside, most of my potential criticisms were already voiced above and I encourage you to take them into consideration first.</p>
<p>Overall I am kind of onboard with the general idea you have here, except for one factor, that being that the ending doesn't deliver. One of the best things you can do in an SCP is turn it on its head with new information which the Foundation either finds post-containment or is relayed to the reader at the end of the article. By way of example, think of <a href="/scp-5915">SCP-5915</a>, which is fairly short but manages to present a simple concept and then add an extra layer of weird at the end when you realize the brains are acting like hermit crabs.</p>
<p>You've mentioned that the bathroom acts like a digestive tract, so that seems like the obvious place to jump off for this extra, end-of-article layer of weird. Whose digestive tract is it? What's going on beneath the surface? Is it sentient/sapient, and is it aware of the people who are using it? If you can find answers to some of those questions, then this can definitely work as a snappy little article.</p>

							</div>

								
